---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Where to Document - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="documentation" subChapterId="where-to-document">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Where you put your documentation matters as much as what you write. AI tools
				have different levels of access depending on where files live.
			</p>

			<p>
				Working{" "}
				<a href="/requirements-gathering/documentation-first-development" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">documentation-first</a>{" "}
				only works if the AI can actually reach the docs. That points to a clear default:
				the source of truth belongs in the repo, next to the code, where every AI session
				can read it without setup.
			</p>

			<h3>In Your Repository (Best Option)</h3>

			<p>
				Documentation that lives alongside your code is the most accessible to AI coding
				tools. When you open a project in Claude Code or Cursor, the AI can read any file
				in the repo. No configuration needed, no plugins required.
			</p>

			<p>
				This makes your repository the best home for documentation that the AI needs
				during development: architecture decisions, approach documents, runbooks, and
				project setup guides. Files like <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>AGENTS.md</code>,
				and docs in a <code>/docs</code> folder are loaded or easily referenced by the AI
				in every conversation.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border-l-4 border-l-teal-400 pl-4 py-2">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800">
						Project root files (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md)
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mt-1">
						These are automatically loaded into the AI's context. Use them for project
						conventions, tech stack, and instructions that should apply to every
						conversation. Keep them concise.
						See <a href="/task-breakdowns/agents-md">AGENTS.md</a> for details.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border-l-4 border-l-teal-400 pl-4 py-2">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800">
						A /docs folder
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mt-1">
						Use a dedicated docs folder for documents: architecture decision
						records, feature approach docs, API documentation, runbooks, etc. The AI can search and
						read these when it needs deeper context. Organize by topic, not by date.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border-l-4 border-l-teal-400 pl-4 py-2">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800">
						Inline code comments
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mt-1">
						For "why" explanations that are tightly coupled to specific code. Don't
						document what the code does (the AI can read it). Document why it does
						it that way, especially for non-obvious decisions or workarounds.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<Callout variant="success" title="Structure matters">
				<p>
					Set up a clear folder structure and document it in your CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md.
					When the AI knows where to look, it finds what it needs faster. Consider building
					a short skill that defines your docs structure so the AI follows it when
					creating new documents.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
					Example folder structure
				</div>
				<div class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`project/
├── AGENTS.md              # Project conventions, loaded every conversation
├── docs/
│   ├── index.md           # Entry point: links into each folder below
│   ├── decisions/         # Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)
│   │   ├── ADR-001-auth-strategy.md
│   │   └── ADR-002-queue-for-payments.md
│   ├── features/          # Feature approach documents
│   │   ├── authentication.md
│   │   └── billing.md
│   ├── runbooks/          # Operational guides
│   │   ├── deployment.md
│   │   └── incident-response.md
│   ├── external/          # Specs from vendors and other teams
│   │   └── payment-provider-api.md
│   └── api/               # API endpoint documentation
│       └── endpoints.md
├── .claude/
│   └── skills/            # AI skills for your team
└── src/                   # Your application code`}</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				For the shape of the documents themselves, a light convention lets any tool (a search
				layer, an indexer, the AI) read them without repo-specific knowledge. Google's{" "}
				<a href="https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/knowledge-catalog/blob/main/okf/SPEC.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">Open Knowledge Format (OKF)</a>{" "}
				is a minimal, tool-agnostic option: a folder of markdown files, each opening with a
				small YAML frontmatter block. The{" "}
				<a href="/requirements-gathering/documentation-first-development" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">documentation-first chapter</a>{" "}
				walks through it in detail.
			</p>

			<h3>In Confluence or Similar Tools (Next Best)</h3>

			<p>
				Not everything can live in the repo. Product requirements, meeting notes, competitive
				research, and broader strategy documents often live in tools like Confluence or
				Google Docs. These are still valuable to the AI, but access requires extra setup.
			</p>

			<p>
				The best way to connect these tools is through CLI tools or MCP servers. Atlassian's
				CLI, for example, lets AI coding tools read Confluence pages and Jira tickets
				directly. MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers provide a standardized way to give
				AI tools access to external data sources.
			</p>

			<p>
				If CLI or MCP access isn't available, you can still reference external docs by
				copying the relevant sections into your repo, for example under <code>docs/external/</code>,
				so the AI can read them during development. A local copy also versions the spec
				alongside your code, so you can see what your integration assumed when the source
				changes. It's not as seamless as a live connection, but it works.
			</p>

			<p>
				Even for documents you don't copy into the repo, leave a pointer to them. Keep a
				README or an <code>index.md</code> in your <code>docs/</code> folder that lists what
				lives outside the repo and where to find it: "Billing requirements live in Confluence
				at [link]," "The partner API contract is in the shared drive under [path]." The AI
				can't open those on its own, but it can see that they exist and ask you to paste or
				retrieve the relevant part instead of guessing. A known gap it can ask about beats an
				unknown one it fills with assumptions.
			</p>

			<h3>What Not to Do</h3>

			<div class="space-y-3 my-8">
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Don't scatter docs randomly</strong>.
						If some docs are in the repo root, some in /docs, some in /wiki, and some
						in random subdirectories, neither humans nor AI will find what they need.
						Pick a structure and stick to it (you can document this as well).
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Don't rely solely on external tools</strong>.
						If your documentation only exists in Confluence and you haven't set up CLI
						or MCP access, the AI can't see it during development. At minimum, keep
						a summary of key decisions and architecture in the repo.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Don't put everything in CLAUDE.md</strong>.
						This file is loaded into every conversation, so it should stay short and
						focused on essentials. Longer documents belong in <code>/docs</code> where
						the AI can read them when needed.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					Audit where your team's documentation currently lives. If important context
					is trapped in Slack threads, Google Docs, or people's heads, pick the highest
					value items and move them into the repo. You don't need to migrate everything
					at once, just start with the documents that would prevent the most repeated
					AI mistakes.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
